  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Chance for afternoon and early evening \nshowers/thunderstorms today, mostly north of the Downeast coast.\nAreas of dense fog possible again tonight though coverage is \nuncertain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nSatellite imagery shows an area of fog along the Downeast coast\nwhich will move inland later this afternoon and evening and \nthen expand north overnight. Patchy/areas of fog will also form \nacross the remainder of the forecast area overnight, \nparticularly where rain fell this afternoon. Dense fog is \npossible which could impact travel, though the extent of \ncoverage is uncertain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Thunderstorms are possible Friday afternoon and\nevening, some of which may be strong with gusty winds and small\nhail.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front crosses the area Friday on the leading edge of a \nfull latitude trough pushing into the region, with the added \nsupport of a northern stream shortwave pushing through the \ntrough around that time. With the potential for 500-1500 J/kg of\ninstability, 25-35KT of bulk shear and elongated, straight\nhodographs, there is a risk of some locally strong \nthunderstorms ahead of the front Friday afternoon, with the main\nthreat expected to be strong gusty winds. The greatest chance \nfor storms is likely to be concentrated over northern and \ncentral areas while more southern areas are likely to remain too\nstable due to southerly flow bringing in marine air. The inland\nextent of the marine layer will be key to watch for determining\nhow far south the severe threat extends.",
